   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result of Section 4 (2026-10-02; $[3,1,0]^2$, 25 values of $t$, extrapolation error $\\le10^{-23}$)\n**Yes.** The copy-dependent ($\\sqrt D$) coefficients are affine in a single transfer eigenvalue at a special point,\n$$\\tau_c=\\text{eigenvalue of }t_2(u)\\text{ at }u=\\theta_1+h\\quad(\\text{untwisted; equivalently }u=\\theta_2+h),$$\nonce they are normalized *per unit signal*. Verified exactly at all 25 $t$ (fit and held-out) for both copies, to $\\le10^{-22}$:\n- $[4,3,1]$:\n  $$\\frac{K_{(3,2)}}{S}=\\frac{19t(t-1)-\\tau_c}{27t},\\qquad\\frac{K_{(1,1)}}{S}=\\frac{4t(t-1)(3t-23)-(3t-11)\\tau_c}{1296t},\\qquad\\hat s_{(2,2)}=\\frac{4t(t-1)-\\tau_c}{24t}.$$\n- $[5,2,1]$ (already clean): $\\hat s_{(1,1)}=\\dfrac{4(t-1)^2-\\tau_c}{168t}$ and $\\hat s_{(2,2)}=6\\hat s_{(1,1)}$.\n- **The per-copy clean combination on $[4,3,1]$ in Q-data:**\n  $$v_c=48\\,[19t(t-1)-\\tau_c]\\,d_{(1,1)}-[4t(t-1)(3t-23)-(3t-11)\\tau_c]\\,d_{(3,2)}.$$\n  Its contamination is $\\le7\\times10^{-21}$, and its signal is $\\ge0.026$, on both copies at all $t$. Add $6d_{(1,1)}-d_{(2,2)}$ for the second direction.\n- **The eigenvalue itself:** $\\tau_1+\\tau_2=8(t^2+2t-1)$ and $\\tau_1\\tau_2=16(t-1)t(t+1)(t+4)$ on $[4,3,1]$, so $\\tau_c=4\\big[t^2+2t-1+\\sigma_c\\sqrt{1+3t^2}\\big]$. On $[5,2,1]$, $\\tau_1+\\tau_2=8t^2+19t-8$ and $\\tau_1\\tau_2=4(t^2-1)(t+4)(4t+3)$.\n- **Negative results** (Section 4 table):\n  - No target is proportional to a single eigenvalue, or to a ratio of two, among the 39 candidates.\n  - The normalized $\\hat k_{(1,1)}=K_{11}/K_{32}$ is a **Möbius** function of $\\tau_c$ (a ratio of the two affine forms), which is why it looked complicated.\n  - $t_1$ never separates copies: its copy-dependent part is the quadratic Casimir.\n- **Not derived:** why $u=\\theta_1+h$ (and not, say, $\\theta_1-3h$, where the insertions sit) gives the simplest coefficients. Other $t_2$ points also give affine relations, since any copy-separating eigenvalue does, but with higher-degree $a(t)$ and $b(t)$.",
